<h2>How the SDR Activity Goal Calculator works</h2><p>The calculator works backward through two funnel stages. Conversations needed = target meetings ÷ conversation-to-meeting rate. Activities needed = conversations needed ÷ activity-to-conversation rate. Dividing by working days turns the monthly requirement into an average daily activity goal.</p><h2>How to use this sdr activity goal calculator</h2><p>Use conversion rates from the same segment, channel and SDR motion you are planning. A cold-call live-conversation rate should not be applied to personalized email or event follow-up. Define “completed meeting” consistently and decide whether no-shows are included in the conversion stage or tracked separately.</p><h2>How to interpret the result</h2><p>The result is a capacity scenario, not a quota recommendation. Very high required activity per day can reveal that the conversion assumptions or meeting target are unrealistic, but simply raising activity volume may reduce personalization and conversion. Use it alongside available selling time and rep workload.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>Conversion rates change with list quality, geography, persona, season, brand awareness and rep skill. The model assumes rates remain stable when activity increases. It also ignores multi-touch sequences where several activities contribute to one conversation. For operational planning, measure sequence-level conversion and channel mix.</p><h2>Practical example and workflow</h2><p>To create 20 meetings with a 25% conversation-to-meeting rate, an SDR needs about 80 live conversations. At an 8% activity-to-conversation rate, that implies around 1,000 activities, or 50 per day across 20 working days. The manager can then test whether the daily load fits available time.</p>
